@h1 A report with live data h1@

Build a release note or status report whose version number, contributor list,
and metrics table all come from @strong outside strong@ the document — programs
and files resolved at build time instead of pasted in by hand. You write the
prose once; the numbers refresh themselves every time you build.

This recipe is for writers, with a developer wiring the data sources once.

@h2 Step 1 — Name each input by alias h2@

Inside the document you only ever name an @strong alias strong@. An alias can
never name a command, which is exactly what keeps the document safe to share.

@@@pre
@load meta load@
@load team load@
@load downloads load@
pre@@@

@h2 Step 2 — Wire the aliases h2@

A developer maps each alias to a program in @code wit.sources.json code@. Every
entry has a @code run code@ (the command as an argument array) and a
@code format code@ — @code json code@ or @code csv code@.

@@@pre
{
  "sources": {
    "meta":      { "run": ["node", "scripts/meta.js"], "format": "json" },
    "team":      { "run": ["node", "scripts/team.js"], "format": "json" },
    "downloads": { "run": ["cat", "data/downloads.csv"], "format": "csv" }
  }
}
pre@@@

A JSON source that prints an @strong object strong@ becomes a record; one that
prints an @strong array strong@ becomes a collection; a CSV source becomes a
collection of records keyed by the header row.

@h2 Step 3 — Use a loaded record in prose h2@

Pull a field straight into a sentence with dotted access.

@@@pre
@h1 Release @meta.version h1@

Cut @meta.date on branch @env.WIT_BRANCH.
pre@@@

The built-in @code env code@ source needs no entry in the config — it exposes
environment variables (loaded from an @code .env code@ file when you pass one).

@h2 Step 4 — Iterate a loaded collection h2@

Loop the contributor list into @code li code@ items.

@@@pre
@h2 Contributors h2@

@ul
(each @team as person)
@li @person.name — @person.commits commits li@
(end)
ul@
pre@@@

@h2 Step 5 — Drop a CSV straight into a table h2@

A loaded CSV collection renders as a table with no hand-typed rows.

@@@pre
@table |rows @downloads|
pre@@@

@h2 Step 6 — Place an image beside prose h2@

@strong row strong@ is an invisible flex band; each @strong col strong@ with a
fixed @code size code@ sits beside flowing text — no float, no clear.

@@@pre
@row
  @col |size 260|
    @img |src assets/downloads.svg| |alt Downloads chart|
  col@
  @col
    macOS leads this cycle, with Windows a steady second and Linux climbing.
  col@
row@
pre@@@

@h2 Step 7 — Build with the sources config h2@

Point the build at the config, hand it the environment file, and grant it
permission to run the programs with @code --allow-exec code@.

@@@pre
wit build report.wit --sources wit.sources.json --env .env --allow-exec --fragment
pre@@@

@strong Result. strong@ A document that opens with
@@@code <h1>Release 0.4.0</h1> code@@@ and
@@@code <p>Cut 2026-07-05 on branch main.</p> code@@@, a contributor list
(@code TauraJ — 42 commits code@, @code Ada Lovelace — 17 commits code@), a
downloads table with header-derived columns, and the image-beside-prose band —
all verified from the worked example. Swap @code --fragment code@ for
@code -o report.pdf code@ to get a styled PDF.

@h2 The trust boundary h2@

The document names only an alias. Only the operator's @code wit.sources.json code@
maps that alias to a command, and @code --allow-exec code@ must be passed for any
program to run. Without it, only the built-in @code env code@ source resolves.
That indirection @em is em@ the security model — a document you receive can never
run a program you didn't wire yourself.

@h2 Pitfalls h2@

@ul
@li @strong Forgetting @code --allow-exec code@. strong@ Every alias other than
the built-in @code env code@ silently fails to load without it. li@
@li @strong Noisy sources. strong@ A JSON source must print @em only em@ JSON to
standard output — a stray log line corrupts the parse. li@
@li @strong Stray list wrapper. strong@ An iterated @code li code@ currently renders
inside an auto-inserted paragraph. It is cosmetic and harmless in a browser; note
it rather than fight it. li@
ul@

@h2 Variations h2@

Any program in any language that emits JSON, CSV, or lines works — a Python
script, a SQL client, a compiled binary. Only the value model (records,
collections, scalars) crosses the seam. To embed this same data flow in your own
app without the CLI, see
@a |href /docs/recipes/embed-parser/| Embed the Wit parser a@.

@h2 See also h2@

@a |href /docs/recipes/csv-table/| Load a CSV into a table a@ is a focused
excerpt of this recipe;
@a |href /docs/recipes/embed-parser/| Embed the Wit parser a@ crosses the same
data seam in-process.
